    "1.16": {
      "file": "content/thermo/ch01-b.js",
      "hash": "b83bdcff803d9101a84a799008780018109b41643d963129f52a6d353df53625",
      "deps": [
        "thermo-1.11",
        "thermo-1.13"
      ],
      "idea": "Insulated, every bit of work done on the gas stays in it: $\\tfrac f2 Nk\\,dT = -P\\,dV = -NkT\\,dV/V$, so $\\tfrac f2\\,d\\ln T = -d\\ln V$, i.e. $TV^{2/f}$ is constant \u2014 and $PV = NkT$ turns that into $PV^{(f+2)/f}$ = const. In contact with a bath the same work flows straight out as heat, and $T$ stays put. Molecularly, each molecule bouncing off an advancing piston comes back a little faster; an insulated gas keeps that energy, a conducting one hands it to the walls. Molecules with rotational energy share the gain over more degrees of freedom, so their temperature rises less: $\\gamma$ falls from 5/3 to 7/5."
    },
