    "3.11": {
      "file": "content/thermo/ch03-b.js",
      "hash": "59810917765072aa3c7cac05c7d3381f130aafc21a0658a588edaf12e9a74f68",
      "deps": [
        "thermo-3.10"
      ],
      "idea": "Body, engines and environment form an isolated system. Energy conservation gives $W = (U - U_0) - Q_0$, where $Q_0$ is the heat dumped into the environment, and the entropy principle (2.10, 3.10) requires $Q_0/T_0 - (S - S_0) \\ge 0$. So $Q_0 \\ge T_0(S - S_0)$ and $W \\le (U - U_0) - T_0(S - S_0)$; the gap is $T_0$ times the entropy generated. Equivalently, each parcel of heat $dQ = -C\\,dT$ leaving the block at $T$ can run a Carnot engine of efficiency $1 - T_0/T$ (2.5), and the exergy is the integral of these efficiencies \u2014 a Riemann integral, approached from below by any finite sequence of engines."
    },
