    "4.10": {
      "file": "content/thermo/ch04-b.js",
      "hash": "c78657a1831f2e4de371bc8480a3fe49354f1a6d8fd142dd7dfbfb3a2de955dc",
      "deps": [
        "thermo-4.9",
        "thermo-3.10"
      ],
      "idea": "A small cluster is mostly surface: its interface costs free energy proportional to $r^2$, while its interior gains only in proportion to $r^3$, so tiny clusters are uphill (that is why metastable states persist, 4.9). Thermal fluctuations climb the barrier with Boltzmann probability $e^{-\\Delta G^*/kT}$ (minimum principle at fixed $T$ and $P$, 3.10). Because $\\Delta g_v$ grows linearly with supercooling and the barrier goes as its inverse square, the exponent changes by orders of magnitude over a few kelvin \u2014 homogeneous freezing of pure water droplets is negligible at \u221220 \u00b0C and almost instantaneous near \u221238 \u00b0C. Impurities and surfaces lower the barrier (heterogeneous nucleation), which is why ordinary water freezes near 0 \u00b0C."
    },
