    "6.11": {
      "file": "content/thermo/ch06-b.js",
      "hash": "fedf8b9a38df82cc6dcafd4ee5bca4db0255464200acd34e43918d11ebc4150c"
    },
    "6.12": {
      "file": "content/thermo/ch06-b.js",
      "hash": "6bfff6635c764fe154bf23b71fd32df709a3d6cb2134142a2449af8af44da08c"
    },
    "6.13": {
      "file": "content/thermo/ch06-b.js",
      "hash": "e871bc226cdc6d9d4ea5e3ecf849579748366498548a1f77a2dc92c0312ee740"
    },
    "6.14": {
      "file": "content/thermo/ch06-b.js",
      "hash": "2be16ec6d8b8a0d753cf9cbe42a259ee587d365b701fb6cde1948b48c77c240a"
    },
    "6.15": {
      "file": "content/thermo/ch06-b.js",
      "hash": "f09a53cb8889dab88381c7fa007e6d648b909e48e873ebce19d5ee4feee1de89",
      "deps": [
        "thermo-6.1",
        "thermo-6.12"
      ],
      "idea": "The energy of a molecule is $\\frac12mv^2$ plus other terms that do not involve $\\mathbf v$, so the Boltzmann factor (6.1) makes $\\mathbf v$ Gaussian with variance $kT/m$ in each component (the equipartition value, 6.12). The speed density is that Gaussian integrated over a shell of area $4\\pi v^2$: a rising $v^2$ (more phase space) times a falling exponential (Boltzmann factor). Maxwell obtained the same law in 1860 from the demand that the components be independent and the distribution isotropic; collisions are what drive a gas to it."
    },
    "6.16": {
      "file": "content/thermo/ch06-b.js",
      "hash": "c5307b502b0895c9fb184ed52e505e7e3b32568cbc2e3ea2e7421154e3de9f84",
      "deps": [
        "thermo-6.8",
        "thermo-5.12"
      ],
      "idea": "Independent atoms give a product of single-atom sums (6.8); the single-atom sum is the phase-space integral $\\int d^3x\\,d^3p\\,e^{-p^2/2mkT}/h^3=V/\\lambda^3$ (a Gaussian integral). The $1/N!$ corrects for the indistinguishability of identical atoms, which is what restores extensivity of $S$ (Gibbs paradox, 5.12); with Stirling's formula $\\ln N!\\approx N\\ln N-N$ it produces $V/N$ inside the logarithm. The constant $h$ is the only trace of quantum mechanics."
    },
    "6.17": {
      "file": "content/thermo/ch06-b.js",
      "hash": "d4ce247068fb3921b6ed67941e19fc9699e957c55bc2fa0a1dcf9d1e30035c0c"
    },
    "6.18": {
      "file": "content/thermo/ch06-b.js",
      "hash": "72a8a45ea0c5e32b454b4a24d92e38bdc4c5e714a765f624845124faec7b896a"
    },
    "6.19": {
      "file": "content/thermo/ch06-b.js",
      "hash": "4859def30fecda2f94c9f0d024c5edc0252ebe3ab67625448058ab7484cbc786"
    },
    "6.20": {
      "file": "content/thermo/ch06-b.js",
      "hash": "0ceab9eeb3077f46ff84576b2b3422c1f5e64d4edfbf93e241ff7afe822a6638"
    },
    "6.1": {
      "file": "content/thermo/ch06.js",
      "hash": "ff013f45867e784f224ae763c53042b8d71ab16787d81cdc9617cec6d4950468"
    },
    "6.2": {
      "file": "content/thermo/ch06.js",
      "hash": "93e8eb448f072ef3fc1fca709ba7835691e3829673712b6f65d6bb521cf5388f"
    },
    "6.3": {
      "file": "content/thermo/ch06.js",
      "hash": "3b670b6464ff3b9206699aa951492455b5e7117eb700c2200e0828e774222153",
      "deps": [
        "thermo-6.2"
      ],
      "idea": "Differentiating $Z=\\sum e^{-\\beta E_s}$ brings down a factor $-E_s$ from each term, and dividing by $Z$ is exactly the Boltzmann-weighted average: $-\\partial_\\beta Z/Z=\\langle E\\rangle$. A second derivative brings down $E_s^2$, giving $\\langle E^2\\rangle$, so $\\partial_\\beta^2\\ln Z$ is a variance; and $C=dU/dT=-k\\beta^2\\,\\partial_\\beta U$ gives the second form."
    },
    "6.4": {
      "file": "content/thermo/ch06.js",
      "hash": "80ee06558e07acb595ff9d7ab4791e85c1919941be83e7e032dbbb0c1796971c",
      "deps": [
        "thermo-6.3"
      ],
      "idea": "From 6.3, $\\sigma_E^2=\\partial^2_\\beta\\ln Z=-\\partial U/\\partial\\beta$, and $-\\partial U/\\partial\\beta=kT^2\\,\\partial U/\\partial T=kT^2C_V$. Heat capacity, an equilibrium response, therefore equals a fluctuation of the very quantity it measures: a fluctuation\u2013response relation, the simplest of its kind. Because $\\ln Z$ of $N$ independent parts is a sum of $N$ terms, its derivatives grow like $N$, so $\\sigma_E/U\\sim N^{-1/2}$."
    },
    "6.5": {
      "file": "content/thermo/ch06.js",
      "hash": "0ff252894e74fbe8d6cf9144a80338c7818214e60589b24cdb7dbed945b0b70c"
    },
    "6.6": {
      "file": "content/thermo/ch06.js",
      "hash": "1f90f016a819f753fdd844e160339bf4f54cb44ca702a13b06a05fa2325d9cde"
    },
    "6.7": {
      "file": "content/thermo/ch06.js",
      "hash": "c9b87e8e43e05e4d0c5867b265e0263f476ad315fcec6416dcb7487c1cacd845"
    },
    "6.8": {
      "file": "content/thermo/ch06.js",
      "hash": "13377fdd7f04795fcf00aba795f4e8ed0927b395bf3060d18f87e184081fae96",
      "deps": [
        "thermo-6.2"
      ],
      "idea": "The exponential turns a sum of energies into a product of Boltzmann factors, $e^{-\\beta(E_a+E_b)}=e^{-\\beta E_a}e^{-\\beta E_b}$, and a double sum of a product of factors is a product of sums. The probabilities factor too, so independent parts are statistically independent. Any coupling $W_{ab}$ spoils the factorization by the extra factor $e^{-\\beta W_{ab}}$. For $N$ identical independent parts this gives $Z=Z_1^N$ (and $Z_1^N/N!$ for indistinguishable ones, 6.16)."
    },
